course: dictionary
lesson: 5621
title: Likely: chance and suitable
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Likely» tells you how big a chance something has of happening. «It's likely to rain» means rain is probably coming. Likely is more than a guess, but it is not certain.
explain: «Likely» can also mean suitable or seeming right, as in «a likely place» for a picnic. Here it has nothing to do with chance. Look at the words around it to tell which meaning fits: ask, does it mean probably, or does it mean suitable?
explain: As an adverb, likely means probably: «She will most likely win.» More likely and most likely compare chances, with most likely the strongest. Unlikely and «not likely!» show the chance is small, and «as likely as not» means about a fifty-fifty chance.
fact: Likely means probably going to happen or be true.
fact: In maths, words show chance: impossible, unlikely, even chance, likely, certain.
fact: «Likely» means more than half the chance, so something likely to happen probably will happen.
fact: «Likely» can also mean suitable, as in «a likely place».
fact: «More likely» and «most likely» compare chances: «running makes you more likely to trip and fall».
exercise: «It's likely to rain this afternoon.» What does likely mean here? | Suitable for a picnic | Very large and heavy | *Probably going to happen | Cold and windy
exercise: «She will most likely win.» In this sentence, what does likely mean? | Certainly, with no doubt at all | Quickly | *Probably | Unfairly
exercise: Sam says, «This is a likely place for our picnic.» What does likely mean? | *Suitable, a place that seems right | Very large | Probably going to happen | Wet and cold
test: Which word shows more than half the chance of something happening? | Never | *Likely | Impossible | Unlikely
test: «Running makes you more likely to trip and fall.» What does more likely mean? | Exactly the same chance | *Greater chance | Slower | Less chance
exam: Which word means the chance is small? | *Unlikely | Probably | Likely | Most likely
